## The results: 115 votes and a more legitimate decision

- **115 votes** in total, 12.6% of the 916 members entitled to vote
- **85 votes on paper** from members in the room
- **30 votes online** from members following from home

Those 30 online votes are more than a quarter of the total, and a **35% increase** on the votes cast in the room. They came from members who could not be in the room, and who an in-person-only assembly would have left out.

The experience was positive and simple, for the members voting from home and for the team running the assembly. And the cooperative got what it was looking for: **a decision with more participation and more legitimacy**, reached through a process that is secure, verifiable and anonymous.

## Why online voting matters for consumer cooperatives

Consumer cooperatives are built on one principle: **one member, one vote**. As they grow, keeping that principle real gets harder.

A cooperative with hundreds or thousands of members cannot fit them all in one room, and the larger the territory its members live in, the fewer of them can attend in person. Members spread across a city, a region or a country face travel time and costs. Members with jobs, shifts or care duties often can't attend at all. The result is an assembly where the decisions are taken by the few who could be there, not by the membership.

Online and hybrid voting change that:

- **More members take part**, because voting takes a few minutes from any device, wherever they are
- **Decisions gain legitimacy**, because the result reflects a broader share of the membership
- **The process stays rigorous**, with verified members, anonymous votes and results anyone can check
- **The assembly keeps its in-person character**, because members who want to attend can still vote in the room

This applies to food cooperatives like FoodcoopBCN, and also to energy, housing, consumer and credit cooperatives, where memberships run into the thousands. It is the same approach that [Plataforma per la Llengua uses to give its members across four states an equal vote](/blog/how-plataforma-per-la-llengua-runs-online-voting-for-an-association-with-members-across-four-states), and the one [COIB used to run its hybrid annual general meeting](/blog/how-coib-a-professional-body-of-nurses-ran-its-2025-annual-general-meeting-vote-online-securely-and-with-instant-results).

If your cooperative runs general assemblies, board elections or member consultations, see how Vocdoni works as [online voting software for cooperatives](/solutions/cooperatives).

## Bring every member to the assembly

FoodcoopBCN's general assembly shows what hybrid voting looks like for a cooperative:

- **Reach every member**, in the room or at home
- **Keep every vote anonymous**, with results anyone can verify
- **Run it yourself**, from the census to the final result
- **Gain legitimacy**, with decisions backed by more of the membership

